{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_spfkgs722ez6ngsb4smvxbc2vb3lodtaebhgtc2abtv3qnfpefiq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Root cause found and it *is* reproducible.** In C, `c2Proxy pA, pB;` are uninitialised locals — fixed stack slots reused by every `c2GJK` call from the same site, so `verts[k]` beyond `count` retains the previous call's data. I verified this by hand-computing: priming with AABB `{(100,200),(300,400)}` then reading `iA[0]=1,2,3` yields exactly the probe's `(5.769,3.846)`, `(4.2,5.6)`, `(1.47,5.88)` — i.e. corners 1,2,3 of the *previous* AABB. Rust zero-initialises instead. Fixing:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":170147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":492,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ed8914d-6817-4736-8f37-e5881dd5b50c","uuid":"4c1f9326-1801-4f52-95f3-6f82a7041cfc","timestamp":"2026-09-05T06:58:32.193Z"}
